@attribute [Route(pageUrl)]
@attribute [Route(pageUrl + "/{Sample}")]
@attribute [Route(pageUrl2)]
@layout DemosMainLayout

<SamplePageHeader ChartKey="@SampleChartKeys.BarChart" Sample="@Sample">
    <SiteMetaTags PageUrl="@pageUrl" Title="@metaTitle" Description="@metaDescription" ImageUrl="@imageUrl" />

    <PageHero Title="@pageTitle">
        <SubTitleTemplate>
            @((MarkupString)pageDescription)
        </SubTitleTemplate>
    </PageHero>

    <DocsLink Href="@DemoRouteConstants.Docs_BarChart" />

    <Prerequisites PageUrl="@pageUrl" />
</SamplePageHeader>

@* Each SampleSection renders only on its own sample page, /demos/<chart>/<slug>. *@
<CascadingValue Value="@SampleCatalog.Find(SampleChartKeys.BarChart, Sample)">
<SampleSection Slug="how-it-works">
    <Block>
        The <strong>Bar Chart</strong> component visualizes data as vertical bars, making it easy to compare values across categories or track changes over time.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Add the <code>BarChart</code> component to your page.</li>
                <li>Provide <code>Labels</code> for the X-axis categories and one or more <code>Datasets</code> for the Y-axis values.</li>
                <li>Customize appearance and behavior using the <code>Options</code> property (e.g., colors, tooltips, axis settings).</li>
                <li>Refer to the demo code for examples of basic and advanced usage, including multiple datasets and custom styling.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
        This demo showcases the essential setup for a bar chart and demonstrates how to bind your data for quick visualization.
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_01_Examples)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="playground">
    <Block>
        Change the main options of a bar chart and watch the chart and its C# update together. The controls cover bar direction, stacking, corner radius and bar width, plus the legend, the locale and right-to-left layout.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Change a control. The chart is updated with <code>UpdateAsync</code>, so it animates from its current state.</li>
                <li>Pick a <strong>Locale</strong> to see how numbers are formatted for that region, and switch on <strong>Right to left</strong> to mirror the legend and tooltip.</li>
                <li>Copy the C# below the chart. It lists only the settings that differ from their defaults, with the property names the library uses.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <BarChart_Playground />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="click-event">
    <Block>
        Set the <code>OnClick</code> callback to react when a chart data item is selected. The callback receives <code>ChartClickEventArgs</code> with the dataset index and label, data-item index and label, and raw value.
        The dedicated demo displays the selected item below the chart.
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_11_Click_Event)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="combo-bar-line">
    <Block>
        The <strong>Combo bar/line</strong> demo mixes <code>BarChartDataset</code> and <code>LineChartDataset</code> in the same <code>BarChart</code>.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Use the <code>BarChart</code> component as the root chart.</li>
                <li>Add both <code>BarChartDataset</code> and <code>LineChartDataset</code> instances to the same <code>ChartData.Datasets</code> collection.</li>
                <li>Configure interaction options such as <code>Mode = InteractionMode.Index</code> and <code>Intersect = false</code> for a combined tooltip experience.</li>
                <li>Refer to the demo code below for a working example with bar columns and a line overlay.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_09_Combo_Bar_Line)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="horizontal-bar-chart">
    <Block>
        The <strong>Horizontal Bar Chart</strong> displays data values as horizontal bars, making it ideal for comparing categories with long labels or when you want to emphasize comparison between values.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Use the <code>BarChart</code> component and set the <code>IndexAxis</code> option to <code>'y'</code> to render bars horizontally.</li>
                <li>Provide your data and labels as you would for a standard bar chart.</li>
                <li>Customize colors and appearance using the available palette utilities or your own color set.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
        Refer to the demo code below for a working example and configuration options.
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_02_Horizontal_BarChart)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="stacked-bar-chart">
    <Block>
        The <strong>Stacked Bar Chart</strong> allows you to display multiple data series stacked on top of each other, making it easy to compare the total and individual contributions of each series for every category.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Use the <code>BarChart</code> component and configure the <code>Options.Scales.X.Stacked</code> and <code>Options.Scales.Y.Stacked</code> properties to <code>true</code> to enable stacking.</li>
                <li>Provide multiple datasets in your chart data, each representing a different series to be stacked.</li>
                <li>Customize colors for each dataset using palette utilities or your own color set for better distinction.</li>
                <li>Refer to the demo code below for a working example and further configuration options.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_03_Stacked_BarChart)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="stacked-bar-chart-with-groups">
    <Block>
        The <strong>Stacked Bar Chart with Groups</strong> displays multiple independent stacks side by side for each category. Datasets that share the same <code>Stack</code> value are stacked together, while datasets with different <code>Stack</code> values are rendered as separate groups.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Enable stacking by setting <code>Options.Scales.X.Stacked</code> and <code>Options.Scales.Y.Stacked</code> to <code>true</code>.</li>
                <li>Assign the same <code>Stack</code> value to datasets that should be stacked together.</li>
                <li>Use different <code>Stack</code> values to render multiple stacked groups side by side for each category.</li>
                <li>Refer to the demo code below for a working example with two grouped stacks and multiple series in each stack.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_10_Stacked_BarChart_With_Groups)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="stacked-bar-chart-with-data-labels">
    <Block>
        The <strong>Stacked Bar Chart with Data Labels</strong> enhances the standard stacked bar chart by displaying value labels directly on each bar segment. This makes it easier to read and compare the values of each dataset within a category.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Use the <code>BarChart</code> component and enable stacking by setting <code>Options.Scales.X.Stacked</code> and <code>Options.Scales.Y.Stacked</code> to <code>true</code>.</li>
                <li>Add multiple datasets to your chart data to represent different series.</li>
                <li>Enable data labels by passing the <code>ChartDataLabels</code> plugin when calling <code>InitializeAsync</code>: <code>plugins: new[] { "ChartDataLabels" }</code>.</li>
                <li>Customize label styling with <code>Options.Plugins.Datalabels</code> or per-dataset <code>Datalabels</code> settings as needed.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
        Refer to the demo code below for a working example and further configuration options.
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_04_Stacked_BarChart_with_Datalabels)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="locale">
    <Block>
        The <strong>Locale</strong> demo shows how to localize number and date formatting in your bar chart.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set the <code>Locale</code> property on the <code>BarChart</code> component to your desired culture code (e.g., <code>"fr-FR"</code> for French).</li>
                <li>Number and date labels in the chart will automatically format according to the specified locale.</li>
                <li>This is useful for displaying charts to users in different regions with appropriate formatting.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
        Refer to the demo code below for a working example and configuration options.
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_05_Locale)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="border-radius">
    <Block>
        The <strong>Border Radius</strong> feature allows you to create bar charts with rounded corners, giving your charts a modern and visually appealing look. This is especially useful for dashboards and applications where aesthetics are important.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Add the <code>BarChart</code> component to your page.</li>
                <li>When defining each <code>BarChartDataset</code>, set the <code>BorderRadius</code> property to a list of pixel values (e.g., <code>new List&lt;double&gt; { 10 }</code>) to control the roundness of the bar corners.</li>
                <li>Optionally, set <code>BorderSkipped = false</code> to apply the border radius to all corners of each bar.</li>
                <li>Customize other appearance options such as <code>BackgroundColor</code> and <code>BorderColor</code> as needed.</li>
                <li>Refer to the demo code below for a working example and further customization options.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
        Using the <code>BorderRadius</code> property, you can easily enhance the style of your bar charts to better match your application's design.
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_06_Border_Radius)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="animations-delay">
    <Block>
        The <strong>Animations - Delay</strong> demo illustrates how to add a delay to the animation of your Bar Chart, making the chart elements appear with a timed entrance effect. This is useful for drawing attention to the chart as it loads or for creating a more engaging user experience.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Add the <code>BarChart</code> component to your page and define your <code>Labels</code> and <code>Datasets</code> as usual.</li>
                <li>Configure the <code>Options.Animation</code> property by setting the <code>Duration</code> (in milliseconds) and <code>Delay</code> (in milliseconds) to control the timing of the animation.</li>
                <li>Bind your <code>chartData</code> and <code>barChartOptions</code> to the <code>BarChart</code> component.</li>
                <li>Refer to the demo code below for a working example of how to set up and customize animation delays.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
        Animation delays help you create visually appealing transitions and can be tailored to fit your application's style.
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_07_Animations_A_Delay)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="animations-dataset-level-delay">
    <Block>
        The <strong>Animations - Dataset level delay</strong> demo demonstrates how to apply different animation delays to each dataset in your Bar Chart. This allows each dataset to animate in sequence, making comparisons clearer and adding a dynamic effect to your data presentation.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Add the <code>BarChart</code> component to your page and define your <code>Labels</code> and multiple <code>Datasets</code>.</li>
                <li>For each dataset, set the <code>Animation</code> property with specific <code>Duration</code> and <code>Delay</code> values to control when each dataset animates.</li>
                <li>Bind your <code>chartData</code> and <code>barChartOptions</code> to the <code>BarChart</code> component.</li>
                <li>Refer to the demo code below for a practical example of dataset-level animation configuration.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
        Dataset-level animation delays are ideal for highlighting differences between series and creating engaging, stepwise chart reveals.
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_08_Animations_B_DataSet_Level_Delay)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="export-image">
    <Block>
        Export the chart as an image with <code>ToBase64ImageAsync</code>, which returns a base64 data URL you can show, store or send, or save it straight to a file in the browser with <code>DownloadImageAsync</code>.
        If the chart is still animating, both wait for the animation to finish, and the image is streamed from the browser, so Blazor Server needs no larger message size.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Keep a reference to the chart with <code>@@ref</code> and initialize it.</li>
                <li>Call <code>ToBase64ImageAsync()</code> for a PNG data URL, or pass <code>ChartImageType.Jpeg</code> and a quality from 0 to 1.</li>
                <li>Call <code>DownloadImageAsync("file-name.png")</code> to save the image as a file without sending it to .NET.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_12_Export_Image)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="show-and-hide-datasets">
    <Block>
        Show and hide whole datasets or single bars from C#, check whether a dataset is visible and count the visible datasets.
        Visibility changed at runtime, from C# or a legend click, stays in place when <code>UpdateAsync</code> refreshes the data, because datasets are matched by <code>Key</code>, or else by label, and bars by label.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Call <code>HideAsync(datasetIndex)</code> and <code>ShowAsync(datasetIndex)</code>, or <code>SetDatasetVisibilityAsync(datasetIndex, visible)</code>.</li>
                <li>Pass a data index too, for example <code>HideAsync(0, 2)</code>, to hide one bar.</li>
                <li>Read the state with <code>IsDatasetVisibleAsync</code> and <code>GetVisibleDatasetCountAsync</code>.</li>
                <li>Refresh with <code>UpdateAsync(data, options, ChartUpdateMode.None)</code> to apply new data without animation; hidden datasets and bars stay hidden.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_13_Show_Hide_Datasets)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="legend-click-event">
    <Block>
        Set <code>OnLegendClick</code> to react when a legend item is clicked. The callback receives <code>ChartLegendClickEventArgs</code> with the dataset index, the legend text and whether the dataset is hidden after the click.
        Set <code>LegendClickToggles="false"</code> to stop the legend from hiding datasets, for example to open details instead.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Add an <code>OnLegendClick</code> callback to the chart.</li>
                <li>Keep <code>LegendClickToggles</code> at <code>true</code> for the default hide and show, or set it to <code>false</code> to handle the click yourself.</li>
                <li>Use <code>DatasetIndex</code>, <code>Text</code> and <code>Hidden</code> from the event arguments.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_14_Legend_Click_Event)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="hover-event">
    <Block>
        Set <code>OnHover</code> to react when the pointer moves onto a different bar. The callback receives <code>ChartHoverEventArgs</code> with the same item details as <code>OnClick</code>.
        It is called only when the hovered bar changes, and once with a <code>null</code> <code>DatasetIndex</code> when the pointer leaves all bars, so it does not flood Blazor Server with calls.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Add an <code>OnHover</code> callback to the chart.</li>
                <li>Check <code>DatasetIndex</code>: a value means a bar is hovered; <code>null</code> means the pointer left all bars.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_15_Hover_Event)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="programmatic-highlight">
    <Block>
        Highlight bars and show their tooltip from C# with <code>SetActiveElementsAsync</code>, for example to point at a value from a table next to the chart.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Pass one or more <code>ChartActiveElement(datasetIndex, index)</code> items to <code>SetActiveElementsAsync</code>.</li>
                <li>Pass an empty list to clear the highlight and the tooltip.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_16_Programmatic_Highlight)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="update-modes-and-animation-control">
    <Block>
        Choose how <code>UpdateAsync</code> animates with <code>ChartUpdateMode</code>, and control the running animation with <code>StopAsync</code>, <code>RenderAsync</code>, <code>ResetAsync</code> and <code>ClearAsync</code>.
        These map one-to-one to the Chart.js <code>update(mode)</code>, <code>stop()</code>, <code>render()</code>, <code>reset()</code> and <code>clear()</code> methods.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Call <code>UpdateAsync(data, options)</code> for the default animated update, or pass <code>ChartUpdateMode.None</code> to apply the change at once. An animated update moves each matched dataset from its current values to the new ones, and a new dataset grows in. Datasets are matched by <code>Key</code> when set, otherwise by <code>Label</code>; datasets that share a label, or have none, pair in order.</li>
                <li>Call <code>StopAsync()</code> to end any running animation; the chart stays as it was drawn at that moment. Call <code>UpdateAsync</code> again to animate to new values.</li>
                <li>Call <code>ResetAsync()</code> to put the chart back in its starting state, before its first animation, and <code>RenderAsync()</code> to draw that state. Stop any running animation first, or its next frame draws over the reset. Call <code>ResetAsync()</code> and then <code>UpdateAsync(data, options)</code> to regrow the chart from its starting state, as on first render.</li>
                <li>Call <code>ClearAsync()</code> to clear the canvas; <code>RenderAsync()</code> draws the chart again. A running animation draws over the cleared canvas on its next frame, so stop it first or wait for it to end.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_17_Update_Modes)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="multiple-axes">
    <Block>
        Add more axes with <code>Scales.AdditionalAxes</code>, keyed by axis ID, and point a dataset at one with <code>YAxisID</code> or <code>XAxisID</code>. Chart.js works out the direction from the first letter of the ID, so <code>"y1"</code> is a y-axis and <code>"x1"</code> an x-axis.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Scales.AdditionalAxes = new() { ["y1"] = new ChartAxes { Position = "right" } }</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Grid = new ChartAxesGrid { DrawOnChartArea = false }</code> on the extra axis so only one set of grid lines crosses the chart.</li>
                <li>Set <code>YAxisID = "y1"</code> on the dataset that uses the extra axis; the others stay on <code>y</code>.</li>
                <li>For a horizontal chart (<code>IndexAxis = "y"</code>) the values are on the x-axis: add <code>["x1"]</code> with <code>Position = "top"</code> and set <code>XAxisID = "x1"</code>.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_18_Multiple_Axes)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="axis-position-and-reverse">
    <Block>
        Move an axis to another edge with <code>Position</code>, flip its direction with <code>Reverse</code>, or hide it with <code>Display = false</code>. A hidden axis still scales the data; only its labels, ticks and grid lines go.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Scales.X.Position = "top"</code> or <code>Scales.Y.Position = "right"</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Scales.Y.Reverse = true</code> to put the highest value at the bottom, so bars hang from the top.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Scales.X.Reverse = true</code> to list the categories from right to left.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Scales.Y.Display = false</code> to hide an axis.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_19_Axis_Position_And_Reverse)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="tick-options">
    <Block>
        <code>ChartAxesTicks</code> controls how many ticks an axis gets and how their labels look. Chart.js picks the tick spacing itself unless you set <code>StepSize</code> or <code>Count</code>, and skips crowded labels unless you set <code>AutoSkip = false</code>.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>StepSize</code> for the gap between ticks, <code>Precision</code> for the number of decimals, or <code>Count</code> for an exact number of ticks on a value axis; <code>MaxTicksLimit</code> caps the number of ticks.</li>
                <li>Set <code>AutoSkip = false</code> with <code>MinRotation</code> and <code>MaxRotation</code> (degrees) to show every category label, rotated to fit.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Font = new ChartFont { Size = 14, Weight = "bold" }</code> and <code>Color</code> to style the labels.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Mirror = true</code> to draw the y labels inside the chart, <code>CrossAlign = "far"</code> to align them away from the axis, and <code>LabelOffset</code> to move labels along the axis, in pixels.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_20_Tick_Options)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="axis-title">
    <Block>
        Label an axis with <code>ChartAxesTitle</code>. The title is hidden until <code>Display = true</code>, and in this library it defaults to <code>"black"</code> text in a bold 12 px font, where Chart.js uses grey.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Scales.Y.Title = new ChartAxesTitle { Display = true, Text = "Units sold" }</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Color</code> and <code>Font = new ChartFont { Size = 14, Style = "italic" }</code> to style it.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Padding = 10</code> for the same space above and below, or <code>new ChartPadding { Top = 12, Bottom = 0 }</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>TitleAlignment = TitleAlignment.Start</code> or <code>TitleAlignment.End</code> to move it along the axis.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_21_Axis_Title)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="grace-and-bounds">
    <Block>
        <code>Grace</code> adds space beyond the data, <code>Bounds</code> decides whether the axis ends at the data (<code>"data"</code>) or at a whole tick (<code>"ticks"</code>, the Chart.js default), and <code>SuggestedMin</code> / <code>SuggestedMax</code> extend the range only when the data does not already reach them. Value axes in this library start at zero (<code>BeginAtZero = true</code>).
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Scales.Y.Grace = "10%"</code> for a share of half the data range (Chart.js measures it from the middle to each end), or <code>Grace = 5</code> for data units.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Scales.Y.Bounds = "data"</code> to end the axis at the highest value instead of the next tick.</li>
                <li>Set <code>SuggestedMin = -50</code> or <code>SuggestedMax</code> for a range the data can still go past; <code>Min</code> and <code>Max</code> fix it.</li>
                <li>To stack two axes on one edge, give both the same <code>Stack</code> and <code>Position</code>, size them with <code>StackWeight</code>, and set <code>Offset = true</code> for space between them.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_22_Grace_And_Bounds)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="floating-bars">
    <Block>
        A floating bar spans a <code>(start, end)</code> range instead of growing from the base. Use <code>FloatingData</code> instead of <code>Data</code>; each item is a number, a range or <code>null</code>, and Chart.js draws numbers from the base as usual.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>FloatingData = [(-6, 4), (-4, 7), 15, null]</code> on a <code>BarChartDataset</code>; when set, it is sent instead of <code>Data</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>BorderSkipped = false</code> so the border is drawn on all four sides of each floating bar.</li>
                <li>Call <code>AddDataAsync(chartData, "July", new BarChartDatasetData("Temperature range (&deg;C)", 14, 29))</code> to add a range to the dataset with that label.</li>
                <li>Set <code>IndexAxis = "y"</code> for horizontal floating bars, for example a schedule.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_23_Floating_Bars)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="per-corner-border-radius">
    <Block>
        <code>BorderRadiusCorners</code> rounds each bar corner by its own amount, in pixels, and <code>HoverBorderRadiusCorners</code> does the same while the bar is hovered. Chart.js does not round the corners on a skipped edge, which by default (<code>BorderSkipped = "start"</code>) is the base of the bar.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>BorderRadiusCorners = new ChartBorderRadius { TopLeft = 20, BottomRight = 20 }</code>; when set, it is sent instead of <code>BorderRadius</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>BorderSkipped = false</code> so the bottom corners can be rounded too.</li>
                <li>Set <code>HoverBorderRadiusCorners</code> for the corners while the bar is hovered.</li>
                <li>Set <code>BorderColor</code> and <code>BorderWidth</code> to make the rounded outline visible.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_24_Per_Corner_Border_Radius)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="base-and-inflate-amount">
    <Block>
        <code>Base</code> sets the value bars grow from instead of zero, <code>InflateAmount</code> widens each drawn bar by a number of pixels (Chart.js uses <code>"auto"</code> to hide hairline gaps), and <code>PointStyle</code> sets the shape used for the dataset in the legend and tooltip.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Base = 50</code> on a <code>BarChartDataset</code> to draw bars up or down from 50.</li>
                <li>Set <code>InflateAmount = 6</code> for 6 extra pixels on each side, or <code>InflateAmount = 0</code> for none.</li>
                <li>Set <code>PointStyle = "triangle"</code> (or <code>"rectRot"</code>, <code>"star"</code>, ...) on each dataset.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Plugins.Legend.Labels = new() { UsePointStyle = true }</code> so the legend shows those shapes.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_25_Base_And_Inflate_Amount)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="title-and-subtitle">
    <Block>
        <code>Plugins.Title</code> and <code>Plugins.Subtitle</code> draw text outside the chart area; Chart.js places the subtitle between the title and the chart. Both are hidden until <code>Display = true</code>, and in this library their text defaults to <code>"black"</code>.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Plugins.Title = new ChartPluginsTitle { Display = true, Text = "Monthly sales" }</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Plugins.Subtitle = new ChartPluginsSubtitle { Display = true, Text = "January to June" }</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Position</code> to <code>"top"</code>, <code>"left"</code>, <code>"bottom"</code> or <code>"right"</code>, and <code>Align</code> to <code>"start"</code>, <code>"center"</code> or <code>"end"</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Padding = new ChartPadding { Top = 10, Bottom = 20 }</code> for space around the title, and <code>FullSize = false</code> to size it to the chart area instead of the canvas.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_26_Title_And_Subtitle)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="tooltip-options">
    <Block>
        <code>Plugins.Tooltip</code> chooses which items the tooltip lists and how it looks. Its <code>Mode</code> and <code>Intersect</code> override <code>Interaction</code> for the tooltip only; Chart.js uses <code>Nearest</code> with <code>Intersect = true</code> by default.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Plugins.Tooltip = new ChartPluginsTooltip { Mode = InteractionMode.Index, Intersect = false }</code> to list every dataset at the hovered label.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Position = "average"</code> (the Chart.js default) or <code>"nearest"</code> to place the tooltip.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Padding</code>, <code>CornerRadius</code>, <code>BorderColor</code> and <code>BorderWidth</code> to style the box, and <code>MultiKeyBackground</code> for the fill behind each color box.</li>
                <li>Set <code>BoxWidth</code>, <code>BoxHeight</code> and <code>BoxPadding</code> to size the color boxes, or <code>UsePointStyle = true</code> to draw each dataset's <code>PointStyle</code> instead.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_27_Tooltip_Options)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="layout-padding">
    <Block>
        <code>Layout</code> adds empty space inside the canvas, around the legend, axes and chart. The dashed borders show the canvas edges.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Layout.Padding = 20</code> for the same padding on every side, in pixels.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Layout.PaddingSides = new() { Left = 60, Top = 10 }</code> for a value per side; when set, it is sent instead of <code>Padding</code>.</li>
                <li>Leave both unset for no padding.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_28_Layout_Padding)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="aspect-ratio-and-resize-delay">
    <Block>
        With <code>MaintainAspectRatio = true</code>, the canvas height follows its width by <code>AspectRatio</code> (width / height); Chart.js uses 2 for bar charts. <code>ResizeDelay</code> waits the given milliseconds after the container stops resizing before the chart redraws, which saves work while the window is dragged; resize the window to see the wide chart catch up.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>MaintainAspectRatio = true</code> (the default) and <code>AspectRatio = 1</code>, <code>2</code> or <code>3</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>ResizeDelay = 300</code> to redraw once, 300 ms after a resize ends; Chart.js uses 0.</li>
                <li>Set <code>DevicePixelRatio = 2</code> to draw twice as many canvas pixels, for sharper exported images.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_29_Aspect_Ratio_And_Resize_Delay)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="hover-and-elements">
    <Block>
        <code>Elements</code> sets defaults for every element of a kind, such as every bar, and a dataset value wins over them. <code>Hover</code> sets which elements highlight under the pointer, separately from <code>Interaction</code>, and <code>Events</code> lists the browser events Chart.js listens to.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Elements = new ChartElements { Bar = new ChartElementsBar { BorderRadius = 8, BorderWidth = 3 } }</code> and leave those values unset on the datasets.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Hover = new ChartHover { Mode = InteractionMode.Index, Intersect = false }</code> to highlight every bar of the hovered label; any value left unset falls back to <code>Interaction</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>HoverBackgroundColor</code> on the datasets to make the highlight easy to see.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Events = ["click"]</code> to react to clicks only: moving the pointer does nothing and <code>OnHover</code> does not fire; clicking a bar still highlights it and opens its tooltip, and <code>OnClick</code> fires.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_30_Hover_And_Elements)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="colors-plugin">
    <Block>
        The Chart.js colors plugin gives each dataset a color from its built-in palette, but only when no dataset sets a color. Bar datasets send no color unless you set one, so the palette applies on its own; line, radar and scatter datasets always send a default color, so there the palette needs <code>ForceOverride = true</code>.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Leave <code>BackgroundColor</code> and <code>BorderColor</code> unset on the datasets to get the palette (<code>Enabled</code> is <code>true</code> in Chart.js).</li>
                <li>Set <code>Plugins.Colors = new ChartPluginsColors { Enabled = false }</code> to turn the palette off.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Plugins.Colors = new ChartPluginsColors { ForceOverride = true }</code> to use the palette even where datasets set their own colors.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_31_Colors_Plugin)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="currency-ticks">
    <Block>
        <code>Ticks.Format</code> formats axis labels with JavaScript's number formatting, so currency, percent and compact labels need no JavaScript of your own. The tooltip value uses the same format.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Locale</code>, for example <code>"en-US"</code>, for the currency symbol and separators.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Scales.Y.Ticks = new ChartAxesTicks { Format = new ChartNumberFormat { Style = "currency", Currency = "USD", MaximumFractionDigits = 0 } }</code>.</li>
                <li>Use <code>Style = "percent"</code> for values between 0 and 1, or <code>Notation = "compact"</code> for labels such as <em>2.5M</em>.</li>
                <li>Chart.js always draws the zero tick as <code>0</code>, without the currency symbol. To format it too, set <code>Ticks.Callback</code> to a registered function instead of <code>Format</code>.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_32_Currency_Ticks_Format)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="legend-click-function">
    <Block>
        A legend <code>OnClick</code> function replaces Chart.js's default toggle, as in Chart.js. The library still keeps the visibility the click changed across <code>UpdateAsync</code>, and still raises <code>OnLegendClick</code>.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>
                    Register the function; call Chart.js's default handler inside it to toggle:
                    <pre><code>blazorexpress.chartjs.registerFunction('demo.toggleOnShiftClick', function (e, legendItem, legend) {
    if (e.native?.shiftKey)
        Chart.defaults.plugins.legend.onClick.call(this, e, legendItem, legend);
});</code></pre>
                </li>
                <li>Set <code>Plugins.Legend.OnClick = new ChartFunction("demo.toggleOnShiftClick")</code>. <code>LegendClickToggles</code> applies only when no function is set.</li>
                <li>Click and Shift-click the legend items; <code>OnLegendClick</code> reports each click with the real hidden state.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_33_Legend_Click_Function)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="stacked-bar-line">
    <Block>
        A <code>BarChart</code> can stack its bars and a line together. Datasets with the same <code>Stack</code> value add up, whatever their type, so a line in the stack runs along the total; a dataset with another value is drawn at its own values.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Options.Scales.X.Stacked = true</code> and <code>Options.Scales.Y.Stacked = true</code>.</li>
                <li>Give the <code>BarChartDataset</code> items that should add up the same <code>Stack</code> value.</li>
                <li>Add a <code>LineChartDataset</code> to the same <code>ChartData.Datasets</code> with the same <code>Stack</code> value: its values are added on top of the bars. Give it another <code>Stack</code> value to draw it at its own values instead.</li>
                <li>Keep the line after the bars in <code>Datasets</code>, and leave <code>Order</code> unset: datasets stack by <code>Order</code>, lowest first, which is the reverse of the order they are painted in, so a line with a lower <code>Order</code> would be stacked first, under the bars.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_34_Stacked_Bar_Line)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="legend-position">
    <Block>
        <code>Legend.Position</code> places the legend on any side of the chart, and <code>Legend.Align</code> moves it along that side. A legend on the left or right leaves more height for the bars.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Options.Plugins.Legend.Position</code> to <code>"top"</code>, <code>"left"</code>, <code>"bottom"</code> or <code>"right"</code>. The default is <code>"top"</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Options.Plugins.Legend.Align</code> to <code>"start"</code>, <code>"center"</code> or <code>"end"</code>. The default is <code>"center"</code>.</li>
                <li>Call <code>UpdateAsync</code> to move the legend on a chart that is already drawn.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_35_Legend_Position)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="datalabels-formatter">
    <Block>
        A datalabels <code>Formatter</code> is a registered JavaScript function that turns each value into its label text. The demo shows every bar as a share of the total.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Add the <code>chartjs-plugin-datalabels</code> script to your page, and pass <code>plugins: ["ChartDataLabels"]</code> to <code>InitializeAsync</code>.</li>
                <li>Register the function shown below in your own script file. The plugin passes it the value and a context with the dataset.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Options.Plugins.Datalabels.Formatter = new ChartFunction("demo.percentOfTotal")</code>. Set <code>Datalabels.Formatter</code> on a dataset instead to format only that dataset.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <CodeChunk Class="mb-3" LanguageCode="LanguageCode.JavaScript" FilePath="~\Pages\Demos\BarChart\BarChart_Demo_36_Datalabels_Formatter_Snippet.js" ProjectRootClassType="DemoAppConstants.ProjectRootClassType" />
    <Demo Type="typeof(BarChart_Demo_36_Datalabels_Formatter)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>
</CascadingValue>

